those hoping that mario party 10 might  finally shake up the series are going to  be a wee bit disappointed you see while  the move to the Wii U has opened up a  few new ways to minigame by the wonder  of the gamepad or a selected group of  amiibo the core game is well exactly the  same as it's always been let's take a  look at the most talked-about addition  here the amiibo on the surface getting  some actual new sounds of amiibos cute  plastic figures of joy there's no bad  thing but there are some limitations the  starters you can only use a selected  group of amiibo with Mario Party 10 for  anything significant anything outside of  that group can only be used in a  read-only capacity to earn Mario points  that can be spent on items in the game  those amiibo that do have full  functionality can be used in the  specific amiibo mode which is largely  the same as the normal Mario Party mode  with a different set dressing here the  smaller board is split into four  sections with whatever amiibo you use  creating the board's theme they're very  lovely they are - at least from a  stylistic standpoint with each themed  board sporting a different set of  power-ups for you to collect and use on  your travel along the circular path  there's a certain amount of strategy  added by having to collect coins and  trade 20 of them in two stars that  appear randomly round the board rather  than just collecting them outright you  can also pick up different tokens along  the way which do things like add more  stars to the board and Amidala coins or  switch a section of the board out for  another themed version from the likes of  peach and Donkey Kong but this is all a  distraction from the fact that the  amiibo mode is essentially just a  smaller version of a full-blown Mario  Party he might be traveling on your  lonesome rather than together in a  vehicle but the end result is the same  well the dice move around the board and  collect as many stars as possible  actually there are some fun minigames to  break up the action which is the biggest  draw of Mario Party 10 and they're part  of a Meebo mode and the more traditional  Mario Party some a more action based  which include platforming challenges and  motion-controlled games others are  slower paced and include memory games  and pattern recognition tests most in  the minigames are a free-for-all but  there are some neat 2v2 challenges to  play through - but what about the  gamepad I hear you cry yes the gamepad  plays a role in the [ __ ] a party mode  for refuse and Bowser amiibo in amiibo  mode one player takes on the role of  Bowser  using the gamepad to play minigames  against the other for players while the  bags of minigames don't exactly sell the  pad as the game changer Nintendo might  have hoped for pretty fun  one of them has he rotating the gamepad  to move fiery bars around while the  other players try and avoid them while  another has you chasing them up a wall  or hammering the triggers it as a party  there are also sections web browser can  attempted to see the other players by  drawing doodles over different paths on  the map or on misspeak rates that may or  may not contain prizes or dangers  weirdly during these moments the games  house YouTube trick the other players  which sort of defeats the objects of the  whole thing but I suppose this way  you're left questioning whether those  helpful doodles are a double bluff or  not so there's a lot of content to play  through in Mario Party 10 but the amiibo  editions and the gamepad bowser games  don't really change the fact that you  played this game many a time before  while there's some fun to be had with  friends we found ourselves getting tired  of the whole thing after just a few  trips around the board  maybe knocking back a few beers along  the way might help but even then Mario  Party is at most only mildly amusing  you
 

